# How the join operator reaches its state, and what its full phase costs.
# Regenerate with -Djoinperf.record=true; see JoinPerformanceGateTest.
#
# The ratios are recorded from a run of the join performance lane, on a runner, and not from a
# developer machine. They do not transfer between the two and the counts do: measured on one run of
# the whole matrix, all thirty-three rows of counts came back identical to what a laptop had recorded,
# while the ratios came back between one and a half and nearly two times higher. The carrier arm is
# held up by serialization and by trips to the layer behind memory and the heap arm is not, so a
# machine that is slower at one and not the other moves the quotient rather than cancelling in it.
# Recording here is what keeps the gate green where it runs: it only fails above what it recorded, so
# a faster machine reads lower and passes, while the runner it guards is held to its own numbers.
# Re-recording on a laptop puts every F row over its threshold on the next run.
# ratio '-' means this row's cost is not held to a threshold, only its counts.
# scenario	tier	batchReads	singleReads	keysRead	writes	ratio
F1	cold	2	0	2000	6000	621.7
F1	hot	2	0	2000	6000	699.4
F1	mixed	2	0	2000	6000	550.6
F2	cold	20	0	20000	42000	138.9
F2	hot	20	0	20000	42000	100.4
F2	mixed	20	0	20000	42000	100.3
I1	cold	5	0	2500	5000	-
I1	hot	5	0	2500	5000	-
I1	mixed	5	0	2500	5000	-
I2	cold	5	0	1665	4165	-
I2	hot	5	0	1665	4165	-
I2	mixed	5	0	1665	4165	-
